Ezekiel 29 starts a new section, oracles against Egypt, but it also continues the larger unit of oracles against the nations. And so to understand it, we might start by looking at some similarities with previous oracles. The opening does not have the 'aha' of chapter 25 or 26 exactly, but it has its own version of hubris, false claims, and explanation in advance of judgement such that nations will know that the LORD is the LORD. So you would do well to remember the findings of the previous instances on Ezekiel 25 through 28.\
But the nature of the false claims the LORD says he will forcibly correct is different here. As is the nature of the greatness of this nation, and the way that greatness will be brought down. There is much to reflect on in the first six verses of Ezekiel 29. We have already here an accusation against the king of Egypt, reason enough on its own to pay careful attention. But while Ezekiel's face is to be set against the king of Egypt, his audience is not the ostensible, grammatical, you that is the king of Egypt. He has a message for the exiles of Judah, and in turn, for us.\
\
