\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The claim in verse 3 is indeed extraordinary.\
But are we translating it correctly? 'My Nile is mine' is clear enough. No other way of interpreting it seems at all likely. But instead of 'I made myself' could we not read 'I made (it, the Nile) for myself or even 'I acted for myself'? The suffix on the verb can be read datively, and the verb can be read as either making or acting.\
But all these readings must in turn be read alongside the crocodile metaphor. We have here a crocodile who either claims to have created himself, or to have created the Nile, or to have acted with the sort of independence only a god has. He is the crocodile of the Nile, who claims ownership of the very river. This is clearly not a local predator, as you say. At the very least, this is a king, claiming more dominion than even a son of Adam, given authority over a whole nation, should be claiming. Moreover, there was a long history of Egyptian kings claiming at least some level of divinity, and Egypt had a crocodile god - but is there any evidence in the text that this is in scope? The Bible does discuss primordial powers of the deep. But there too, is there any evidence in the text that those are in scope? That is, we should both be quite careful to use the text itself as our guide to how to interpret the text, though of course other parts of the Bible can assist with that as well.\
So we have questions outstanding about the crocodile's claims. You already started on looking at the response to those claims, which I think is where we must turn to understand the claims. I agree that removal from the Nile is an answer to the claim to ownership of the Nile - but I think we should be more precise as to what way it is an answer. And of course, my agreement should not be taken too seriously. It is to the text, and the evidence we find in it, that we must turn to answer our questions, to decide if our theories are correct.\
Similarly, yes, the fish do seem to be dependent people and perhaps systems. Though to understand the analogy, it is worth pointing out that fish depend on the Nile. \
Also, it is worth pointing out that this oracle is addressed not just to the king, but to all Egypt. Lastly, it is worth pointing out that we have seen these hooks before, in Ezekiel 19. There the hooks were used on lions, that is kings of Judah. Once the hooks were in the hands of Egypt, once, in the hands of Babylon.\
And yes, this oracle is contemporaneous with the siege of Jerusalem, and it surely interacts with hope by the exiles placed in just such Egyptian intervention, as had happened before.\
Comparisons with Tyre perhaps are better answered with later verses.
