\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 You've raised an interesting question. Are the fish exiles living in Babylon? The people under siege in Jerusalem? The inhabitants of Egypt? More than one of those?\
Regardless of the answer though, the message to the exiles about who commands the Nile seems to be the same.\
As for the reed staff metaphor, let's open up that past history with Egypt and see what the rest of the Bible has to say on the matter. That is the authority to which we must turn.\
Jeremiah 37 records Egyptian army maneuvers of exactly the sort which the exiles might be hoping for - though with a warning that ultimately, this hope would be futile.\
Ezekiel 17 records the LORD's rejection of that hope on the grounds that the LORD decides what happens, and not even the might of Egypt would be of any help because Babylon was sent by the LORD to punish. Moreover, in Ezekiel 17, the LORD says that part of what is being punished is turning to Egypt for help.\
Thus, Egypt is acting as an alternative to keeping covenant with the LORD and with Babylon. A false, unreliable alternative. Egypt may think it can influence events using armies - but it will find that it cannot even protect itself, much less Jerusalem. For its own sake, it must know this. For their own sake, the Jewish exiles living in Babylon must know this. For the sake of the LORD's name, all nations must know this.\
To bring about this knowledge, any sort of dominance over other nations - and for a time, even over themselves - is denied Egypt. And this, I think, has everything to do with the belief that because they have a large army (and for thousands of years, had been able to project power in ways others feared), they are the ones who decide what happens with the nations, not the LORD. The LORD demonstrated once before that he could take a nation of slaves, even from Egyptian territory itself, even on turf claimed by Egypt's gods and protected by Egypt's army. So now, he makes the claim before it happens that he will break that power projection. And so it came to pass.\
While this happened after the dating of Ezekiel 29, Jeremiah 44 continues to warn against putting trust in Egypt, in this case by fleeing there as exiles. Jeremiah 44 explicitly invokes the coming devastation of Egypt as a reason not to flee there for protection, and uses a similar broken reed metaphor. Whether trusting for military aid or trusting that military to make a safe haven to flee to, trusting Egypt is something that the LORD warns against. In the same way Jeremiah 44 parallels the broken reed metaphor, Jeremiah 46 parallels the Nile metaphor. There, Egypt thinks it will rise like the Nile, and thus cover the earth, destroying cities and people. Jeremiah 46 explicitly connects this Nile language, power projection against other nations, trusting in Egypt, and God's people being restored but by the power and protection of the LORD. This is not proof that Ezekiel saw such a connection, but it provides a possible such connection.\
Isaiah, unlike these chapters of Jeremiah, was clearly known to Ezekiel. Isaiah 30 and 31 are written in the context of Assyrian invasion, not Babylonian, but Egypt had been a potential source of false hope for a long time; that is what makes the prediction that its power projection will be broken so striking. Isaiah 30 and 31 also warn that Egypt's help is empty, diagnosing the problem as seeing the world in terms of who has the strongest army, forgetting what God might do. "The Egyptians are man, and not God, and their horses are flesh, and not spirit. When the Lord stretches out his hand,\'a0the helper will stumble, and he who is helped will fall, and they will all perish together."\
The nature of the punishment inflicted may be a further clue. Going again to the text, we find a punishment that closely resembles the punishment God had long threatened against his own people for covenant breaking. Had God's people understood, had they known the LORD, they would not have rebelled. Much of Ezekiel establishes that it is only seeing the power of the LORD punishing them, and that deservedly, that would bring the knowledge of the LORD. Similarly, had Egypt known, they would not have tried to use their military power to stop these covenant curses against the people of God. But they will not know until they themselves receive similar punishment.
