\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 While much can be learned from Torah about how the LORD operates with nations, it is possible to overgeneralize. The Torah in the same breath asserts that all the earth is the LORD's, but also that the LORD's people exactly that, his personal property, a kingdom of priests and a holy nation. Even here, dealing with a superpower, the LORD is explaining his motivations with respect to his people.\
And yet, you are not wrong. The references to Torah invite us to look at the LORD's purposes for Egypt as a nation. And Torah does, as you say, have some discussion about the LORD's plans and dealings with other nations.\
Now, on to verses 17-21.\
I think what you say about the LORD's sovereignty is key here.\
The king of Egypt is working for his own purposes, against the LORD's purposes, and so will fail. The king of Babylon is working for the LORD's purposes, and so not only will succeed, but will be rewarded for his efforts. These both happen by the same means. Egypt is a superpower set against the LORD, so it is humbled. Babylon is a superpower moved around the nations as a hired army might move at the direction of its paymaster, so it is paid.\
However, the timing of verse 21, that day, is perhaps less obvious than it seems.\
Day, in Ezekiel and the Hebrew Bible, is not always quite as simple as a time marker. By the time of this oracle, the day warned of in Ezekiel 7 has already come. The people thought that time or money would keep them from the day of the wrath of the LORD. They had already been told they were wrong, and then proved wrong by events.\
Still, that day could be the day the LORD's wrath against Egypt comes to pass. But what, then, is the connection to the house of David? If not then, when? The return under Cyrus? The coming of Messiah?\
Also, the dating of this oracle seems to preclude a lifting of the earlier restriction on what Ezekiel is permitted to say. But I think you are correct that the open mouth is about Ezekiel's reception by the people. There are so many hints throughout Ezekiel that the people heard, but did not listen. Perhaps events, such as what would happen to Egypt, would turn the people to his ministry. While the exile did not completely remove idolatry from the people, what Ezekiel predicted in terms of cleansing did seem to begin around that time. And yet - perhaps it would not be until the coming of Messiah that Ezekiel's own people would listen to him.\
As for whether Messiah is being discussed at all here, I think our best evidence is Psalm 132. There, we have this exact language of a horn sprouting, and it is specifically for David. As for evidence internal to Ezekiel, this chapter already interacts to some extent with Ezekiel 17. And Ezekiel 17:22 takes the same cedar metaphor used to paint the then current Davidic monarchy as foolish guilty oathbreakers, and affirms that an offshoot of the Davidic line will be installed on a high and lofty mountain, presumably Zion but likely deliberately not named as such.\
Still, even if that line speaks of Messiah, I think you are correct about the chapter as a whole. Ezekiel has spoken in many previous chapters of the LORD who both speaks and performs his word.\
Still, that was clearly not the worldview of his audience. So Ezekiel must do more than assert these things. What is his rhetorical strategy, speaking into a world which does not naturally see the LORD as sovereign, but instead fears and trusts agricultural production, armies, and diplomacy?
