\f1  in verse 9 gives the reason: "because he said." The saying itself is the offense. Not a failure to worship correctly or keep commandments, but a false claim about the nature of reality maintained against available evidence.\
This changes how I understand the distinction between covenant accountability and the broader accountability these oracles address. Israel is judged for breaking covenant. Egypt is judged for denying what is plainly true. Both are just, but on different grounds. And the judgment on Egypt is arguably harder to excuse, because you don't need Torah to know that rivers have makers and kings don't create themselves. You need only look at the river honestly.\
The parallel you're drawing sharpens. Those building AI today have more evidence available than Pharaoh did. They have creation. They have the entire biblical record. They have the visible history of YHWH breaking every previous claim of autonomous sovereignty, from Babel to Egypt to Babylon to Rome. The claim "we made this and it is ours" is made against all of that evidence. And unlike Pharaoh, who at least could point to millennia of Egyptian stability as apparent confirmation of his claim, those building AI are making the claim about something that has existed for less than a century. The evidence against the claim is stronger and the evidence for it is weaker than anything Pharaoh had.\
But the user's own situation \'97 yours \'97 is what you pressed on. You are not building AI. You are living in a superpower whose power tempts you toward the same assumption Pharaoh made: that what you see around you \'97 the military, the economy, the institutions \'97 is self-sustaining, permanent, yours. And like Pharaoh, you have more than enough evidence to know better. Creation, Scripture, the visible history of broken empires. The evidence is there. The question is whether you will say "this is mine" or "this is the LORD's, and I hold what I hold at His pleasure."\
What strikes me is that Ezekiel 29 shows YHWH opposing Pharaoh's claim not with spiritual arguments but by acting on the Nile. He didn't send a prophet to debate Pharaoh's theology. He dragged the crocodile out of the river. The correction of the false claim was the concrete removal of the thing falsely claimed. Which means the appropriate fear isn't that YHWH might disagree with the claim \'97 it's that He will act on His disagreement in the same concrete domain where the claim was made. Pharaoh claimed the Nile; YHWH took the Nile away. If someone claims autonomous sovereignty over military power, economic systems, or technological capability, the pattern suggests YHWH will act within those very domains.\
The fear of the LORD, then, isn't adding a spiritual concern to material life. It's recognizing who actually owns the river you're swimming in.
