\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Yes. The pattern is clear enough from Ezekiel 29. The one who says "this is mine and I made it" doesn't say it because he lacks evidence against it. He says it because everything he can see confirms it. Pharaoh looked at the Nile and saw water that had sustained his civilization for millennia. He looked at his army and saw power that had projected across the ancient world. He looked at his throne and saw a dynasty that had outlasted every challenge. Everything visible said "this is yours." The claim felt like observation, not arrogance.\
That's what makes the blindness both understandable and culpable. Understandable because the visible evidence really does seem to confirm it. Culpable because visible evidence is not all the evidence there is, and Pharaoh had access to more \'97 from creation, from the Exodus, from the plain fact that he did not make the river he claimed.\
Someone with a decisive advantage in AI capability will be in exactly Pharaoh's position. They will look at what they can do and see that no one can match it. They will look at the systems they control and see that opposition is futile. Everything visible will confirm that the balance of power is in their hands, that what they choose will happen. And like Pharaoh, they will be wrong \'97 not because the visible power isn't real, but because the One who commands rivers and nations and the rise and fall of every human capability has not been factored in. Not because He's invisible in the way that undetectable things are invisible, but because acknowledging Him would require abandoning the claim that sustains their confidence.\
The blindness Ezekiel warns about isn't ignorance. It's the refusal to see what would undermine the claim. Pharaoh could have looked at his own nation's history with YHWH and concluded "perhaps the Nile is not mine after all." He didn't, because that conclusion would have dissolved the foundation of his rule. Similarly, whoever holds decisive AI capability could look at the record of every previous claim of autonomous sovereignty and see that YHWH broke every one. They won't, because that recognition would require them to hold their capability as stewardship rather than possession \'97 and stewardship doesn't conquer.\
What haunts me about this chapter is the fish. The fish didn't claim the Nile. They just lived in it. They clung to Pharaoh's scales not by choice but because they were in the water he claimed. And when YHWH dragged the crocodile into the wilderness, the fish went with him and died on the open ground. The judgment on the false claim consumed those who merely depended on the system built on it.\
Which means the danger isn't only to whoever makes the claim. It's to everyone swimming in the water they claim. Everyone whose life, economy, security depends on the systems controlled by whoever says "this is mine." When YHWH acts against the claim \'97 and the pattern says He will \'97 the dependents suffer the same removal from the source of life as the one who falsely claimed it.\
This is not a warning to become less dependent on technology or to retreat to simpler living. That would be another form of trusting in visible arrangements. The warning is that the One who commands rivers commands this too, and that whoever claims otherwise \'97 however understandably, however much the visible evidence seems to confirm it \'97 is making a claim YHWH has broken every single time it has been made. Without exception. Across every civilization that has made it.\
The fear of the LORD would mean holding even genuine, world-changing capability as something received, not made. Something governed, not owned. Something that the One who gave it can remove by the same hooks He used on Pharaoh's jaws. And that fear \'97 not the spiritual supplement to a materialist worldview, but the accurate assessment of who actually determines outcomes \'97 is what Pharaoh lacked, what the builders of Babel lacked, and what whoever next says "nothing can stop me" will lack.\
Until the hooks go in. And then they will know.
