\f1  describes what Egypt currently does \'97 every time Israel turns to Egypt as 
\f2 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1489 \u1456 \u1496 \u1464 \u1495 
\f1 , that turning brings their iniquity to remembrance. The verse says this will no longer happen. Egypt will no longer be powerful enough to function as a false trust, and therefore will no longer occasion the sin that brings iniquity to mind. The positive purpose isn't that a reduced Egypt reminds Israel of past sin. It's that a reduced Egypt removes the ongoing occasion for new sin. The reminder of iniquity is the problem being solved, not a benefit being preserved.\
Second, you said Egypt "did not set itself against the LORD's people." This is largely right \'97 the text frames Egypt as something leaned on rather than as an attacker, and the contrast with the thorny neighbors of Ezekiel 28's conclusion is real. But it may be slightly too clean. Egypt actively encouraged Zedekiah's rebellion against Babylon. Jeremiah 37, which you yourself cited, shows Egypt marching an army toward Jerusalem. Egypt wasn't merely passively available to be leaned on \'97 Egypt was extending the reed, as it were. The text's emphasis falls on Israel's turning rather than Egypt's encouraging, so your reading captures the primary thrust. But the claim that Egypt did not set itself against God's people could use qualification, since encouraging rebellion against the instrument of God's discipline is a form of opposing God's purposes for His people, even if motivated by Egyptian self-interest rather than hostility toward Israel.\
Third, the parallel between Egypt's punishment and covenant curses. The scattering language and the forty years are real and striking parallels. But you presented this fairly strongly \'97 "a punishment that closely resembles the punishment God had long threatened against his own people for covenant breaking." I accepted this and built on it without testing it adequately. The forty years could echo the wilderness wandering, but it could also simply represent a generation, which is a natural period for complete demographic turnover. The scattering verbs are used for Israel but may simply be how YHWH describes exile generally rather than a specific allusion to covenant curses applied to a non-covenant nation. The parallel is suggestive and possibly correct, but we treated it as more established than the evidence warrants. It would need more work \'97 perhaps examining whether Ezekiel uses these specific verbs differently when addressing covenant and non-covenant nations \'97 before it can bear the weight we placed on it.\
Fourth, the Exodus connection. You said Egypt had "an earlier experience by an earlier king of Egypt with the sovereignty of the LORD, which ought to have warned them." This is compelling and I believe correct. But I should note that the text of Ezekiel 29 itself does not explicitly reference the Exodus. The Nile setting naturally evokes it, and the hooks might loosely parallel YHWH's demonstrated power over Egypt's waters. But the connection depends on reading Ezekiel 29 within the broader canon rather than on markers within the text itself. You were careful about this kind of distinction earlier \'97 insisting on textual evidence within the chapter before accepting connections. The Exodus connection is strong canonical reasoning, but it's worth acknowledging that Ezekiel himself doesn't invoke it here the way he invokes Torah elsewhere.\
Fifth, something you did not say that perhaps you should have. You developed the message to the exiles extensively \'97 don't trust Egypt, YHWH commands the Nile, the reed will break. But you did not develop what the oracle means for Egypt itself as a nation before YHWH. Verses 13-14 promise restoration. Egypt will be gathered, returned to Pathros, and exist as a lowly kingdom. This is mercy. Not covenant mercy \'97 Egypt doesn't receive a new heart or a new temple \'97 but genuine mercy nonetheless. YHWH doesn't destroy Egypt. He reduces Egypt to a scale where Egypt can exist without the false claim that brought judgment. You mentioned this in passing but didn't sit with what it reveals about YHWH's disposition toward a non-covenant nation. The recognition formula in verse 6 says all the inhabitants of Egypt will know that He is YHWH. Does that knowledge, combined with restoration, suggest something about YHWH's purposes for Egypt beyond mere humbling?\
Sixth, and more tentatively \'97 throughout the conversation you guided me toward seeing YHWH's sovereignty as operating concretely in every domain. This was right and I needed it. But in your application to the present \'97 the United States, AI, someone claiming "no one can stop me" \'97 you moved fairly quickly from textual evidence to present application without pausing to examine whether the pattern established for ancient Near Eastern superpowers transfers directly to modern conditions. The pattern may well transfer. But earlier in this project you insisted on careful evidence before extending principles. The applications felt more like prophetic urgency than the methodical approach you've used elsewhere. That urgency may itself be appropriate \'97 but it's worth noting the shift.
