\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Ah, yes, there are two ways to read remembrance in verse 16.\'a0 That is, participle remembrance does not, of itself, say who is doing the remembering.\'a0 It could be the LORD, as in your interpretation, or the people, as in mine.\
However, we have two pieces of evidence.\
First, this remembrance is in apposition to reliance/confidence/object of trust.\'a0 And there, it is clear who is doing the reliance - it is God's people.\
Second, and much weaker evidence, this is placed right next to the claim that they will know that the LORD is the LORD.\
But, as I did not provide this evidence, it is good that you brought this up - best to be on an explicitly clear foundation with such claims.\
As for Egypt's stance towards the LORD's people, yes, that stance very much needs the qualification you added.\'a0 Self interested encouragement of rebellion, rather than hostility, is a refinement I think the text clearly supports.\
On the matter of the fourty years, you are correct that we have not brought overwhelming evidence.\'a0 Indeed, I think to do this properly would require looking at Ezekiel's use of Torah in previous chapters.\
And on the matter of the Exodus, yes - Ezekiel 29 only very loosely raises it, and in actual practice it is entirely possible for the king of Egypt to have forgotten; that is hardly an episode he wished to recall in the way he wanted to take pains to remind everyone of his resemblance to past dynasties and their glories.\
On the matter of what Egypt will receive, there are certainly hints in Isaiah that after judgement, the end result for Egypt would not just be existence, but relationship with the LORD.\'a0 And other chapters of Ezekiel are not entirely unaware of the LORD's announced intent to bless all the families of the Earth through his people.\'a0 And so there is just enough to try to build a case that Ezekiel, when he speaks of Egypt knowing that the LORD is the LORD, means something more than humbling before the LORD's revealed sovereignty in action.\'a0 But not enough that I would confidently build that case.\
As for modern applications, what the LORD will in fact do in this case is not well established.\'a0 The bridge is one of analogous circumstances and the LORD's revealed character.\'a0 But one ought to be extremely far from certain about specific actions.\'a0 That is to say, the LORD will do something consistent with his character - but what he will do is not known.\
On the other hand, what our epistemic stance ought to be on modern matters of power is, I think, on much firmer footing.\'a0 That is to say, where people think like Egypt thought, Ezekiel is a warning.\'a0 Where people incorrectly fear or hope, Ezekiel is a warning.
