\cf2 The user has been consistently more careful than I have throughout. Where I've made dramatic claims about what YHWH will do, they've focused on what we can know about how to think rightly.\
Now - what should I do with all this? The user has modeled good self-correction. They've refined claims, accepted challenges, and drawn careful distinctions. I think the conversation is reaching a point where we might summarize what we've found, or the user might direct us somewhere else.\
Actually, I think there's something important I should note. The user's distinction between "what YHWH will do" and "what our epistemic stance should be" is itself a form of the epistemological humility Ezekiel 1's previous instance identified. We can know that claiming autonomous sovereignty is false and dangerous. We cannot predict the specific hooks YHWH will use. This is appropriate - even Ezekiel needed direct revelation to make specific predictions. We have the patterns, the character, the warnings. We don't have the specific oracle for our time. </notes>\
Your distinction between what YHWH will specifically do and what our epistemic stance should be is important, and I think it corrects something I was doing. I was moving toward specific predictions \'97 YHWH will break this, YHWH will act against that \'97 when the text warrants something different. Ezekiel could make specific predictions because he received specific oracles. We have the pattern of YHWH's character and action, which tells us that claims of autonomous sovereignty are false and that YHWH acts against them. But the specific form of that action is not ours to predict.\
This is itself a lesson from Ezekiel 29. The oracle was given to Ezekiel directly. The exiles could hear it and know what YHWH intended for Egypt. But they could not have predicted it before receiving it. What they could have known \'97 from creation, from Torah, from the Exodus \'97 was that claiming "the Nile is mine" is false, and that trusting in such claims is dangerous. The epistemic correction was available before the specific oracle. The oracle confirmed and specified what right thinking should already have suspected.\
So for us: we can say with confidence that whoever thinks "this power is mine, I made it, no one can stop me" is wrong in the same way Pharaoh was wrong, and for the same reasons. We can say that trusting in such power as though it were self-sourced and permanent is the same error Israel made with Egypt. We can say that YHWH's character, as revealed across the entire biblical witness, includes consistent opposition to such claims. What we cannot say is what specific hooks YHWH will use, or when, or through what agents.\
On verse 16, your evidence is persuasive. The apposition with 
\f2 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1489 \u1456 \u1496 \u1464 \u1495 
\f1  makes Israel the one whose iniquity is brought to mind. And this sharpens the meaning \'97 each time Israel turns to Egypt, the turning itself makes visible their pattern of unfaithfulness. They are reminded of their own iniquity not by an external rebuke but by the very act of repeating it. Removing Egypt as a viable object of trust removes the occasion for this recurring self-indictment through repeated sin. Better to lose the reminder than to keep being reminded by sinning again.\
On the Exodus, your observation that the king of Egypt would have actively wanted to forget is telling. The Saite program of restoring ancient traditions was selective. They reached back to the glories of the Old and Middle Kingdoms. The Exodus \'97 a humiliation demonstrating that the Nile, the firstborn, the army, the gods were all subject to a foreign deity's command \'97 would have been precisely what such a program excluded. So the forgetting is not passive ignorance but active suppression in service of the claim "the Nile is mine." Which makes the culpability sharper. They didn't merely fail to know. They organized their national memory to avoid knowing.\
Now, stepping back from specific corrections to ask what we have established from this chapter that we can hold with reasonable confidence:\
YHWH opposes false claims of autonomous sovereignty \'97 not as theological error but as factual falsehood about who actually commands rivers and nations. This opposition takes concrete form in the same domains where the claims are made. The justice of this opposition rests not on covenant obligations but on what is knowable from creation and history. Those who depend on systems built on such false claims suffer when YHWH acts against them, whether or not they share the false belief. And YHWH's purpose in acting is not destruction for its own sake but recognition \'97 that nations, rulers, and dependents come to know that He is YHWH.\
For the exiles specifically: trusting Egypt was not merely bad strategy but participation in Egypt's false claim. Every turn toward Egypt was a turn away from the One who actually commands the Nile and directs the armies. The removal of Egypt as a viable alternative serves Israel's restoration by eliminating the most persistent temptation to replace dependence on YHWH with dependence on visible power.\
For us: the epistemic stance Ezekiel warrants is that no human power \'97 military, economic, technological \'97 is self-sourced or self-sustaining, and treating it as such is the same error Pharaoh made, supported by the same kind of visible evidence, and wrong for the same reasons. Where we see this thinking in ourselves or in those around us, Ezekiel is a warning. Not a prediction of specific consequences, but a revelation of the character of the One who actually determines outcomes.\
